Electricity from the sun is expanding faster than almost any other power source globally. Solar cells turn light straight into current, and mirror-based plants focus heat onto a fluid that spins a turbine. Panel prices have dropped steeply in ten years, so solar now rivals fossil fuels in numerous places. The big obstacle is nighttime, since panels produce nothing without light; grid batteries and pumped-water storage help balance things out. Many governments reward households that put panels on their roofs, and huge solar farms spread across vast stretches of land in sunny nations.